use ../lib/CodingRule

from ./LinuxKernelStyle import LinuxKernelStyle

export coding-rule LinuxKernelReturnValueMatchesName:
    standard: @{LinuxKernelStyle}
    ruleId: 16
    title: Match the success return convention to the function name
    statement:
        If a function's name is an action or command, return an error code:
        0 on success and a negative errno on failure. If the name is a
        predicate, return a boolean that is nonzero when it holds. Exported
        functions must follow this, and public ones should.
    rationale:
        Mixing the two conventions is a common source of hard-to-find bugs,
        and C's types will not catch it.
    exceptions:
        Static functions are only recommended to follow it. Functions that
        return a computed result, such as a pointer, are exempt and usually
        report failure with an out-of-range value, NULL or ERR_PTR.
    severity: null
    noncompliant:
        ```c
        \\\
        /* returns 1 on success, 0 on failure */
        int start_engine(struct engine *e);

        /* returns 0 if the link is up */
        int link_is_up(struct port *p);
        \\\
        ```
    compliant:
        ```c
        \\\
        /* returns 0 on success or -EBUSY */
        int start_engine(struct engine *e);

        /* returns true if the link is up */
        bool link_is_up(struct port *p);
        \\\
        ```
    languages: [c]
    tags: [functions, error-handling, naming]
    source: https://docs.kernel.org/process/coding-style.html#function-return-values-and-names
